<options source="ScorchDroid">
  <!-- M2 vertical slice: a fast local practice-vs-bots game. This is a
       ScorchDroid-owned config, not part of upstream's data/ - see the
       porting plan (AssetDataExtractor / initEngine). -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfMinPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M5 Phase 2: OptionsGame.cpp's own default NoMaxPlayers is 2, which
       addHumanTank() + the single starting bot already fill on their own -
       ServerConnectAuthHandler::processMessageInternal() rejects
       ("Server full") any real network client trying to join once
       getNoMaxPlayers() - getNoOfTanks() <= 0, found the hard way testing
       a real two-emulator join. Raised well past 2 so a host still has
       room for real LAN players, not just its own local bot-practice tank. -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfPlayers</name>
    <value>8</value>
  </option>
  <!-- Despite the name, this is a *target total player count*, not a
       threshold for kicking bots: ServerStateEnoughPlayers::ballanceBots()
       counts humans and AIs together and adds or removes bots until the game
       holds exactly this many (0 disables balancing altogether). So 2 is what
       creates the practice bot - one human plus one added bot - and is also
       why a second human joining drops it again, which the two-device join
       test showed as "(Bot) Fred ... Auto-kick". That is correct, not a bug:
       the bot is standing in for the missing player.
       Raising this would *add* bots rather than preserve them, and it is how
       a "number of players" setting should eventually be built - upstream's
       own settings dialog drives this exact entry. -->
  <option>
    <name>RemoveBotsAtPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M4: engine_jni.cpp's addHumanTank() adds one real (destinationId != 0)
       "my tank" player itself, counted by
       ServerStateEnoughPlayers::countBots() exactly like a real network
       client would be - so only one more bot slot is needed to reach the
       2-player total above. PlayerType1 left as "Human" (the OptionsGame.cpp
       default) so ballanceBots() doesn't also try to add a bot into that
       slot. -->
  <!-- The one entry here that names something a *mod* has to provide, and
       the only such name in this file. The Apocalypse mod does not have
       "Moron" - five of its AI definitions, that one included, sit inside an
       XML comment - so a slot naming it never fills and the game waits
       forever for a bot that cannot be created. GameSetup::
       ensureBotsValidForMod substitutes an AI the chosen mod really provides
       before the session config is written. Anything else added here that
       names a weapon, tank type or AI needs the same treatment. -->
  <option>
    <name>PlayerType2</name>
    <value>Moron</value>
  </option>
  <!-- TurnSimultaneous (0): every tank can move/fire within the same round
       window, rather than strict turn order (upstream's default,
       TurnSequentialLoserFirst). Chosen for pace on a phone, not out of
       necessity - the original note here claimed simultaneous "simplifies
       touch-fire" and implied the sequential modes needed turn-order
       bookkeeping we don't have. That was an M2-era worry and it is not
       true: verified 2026-09-08 by playing a Sequential Loser First game
       from the setup screen, which reached turn 3/15 with the shot clock,
       the Fire button and the bot's replies all behaving. The setup screen
       offers every turn type, so this is a default, not a constraint. -->
  <option>
    <name>TurnType</name>
    <value>0</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>ShotTime</name>
    <value>35</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>BuyingTime</name>
    <value>30</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>StartTime</name>
    <value>5</value>
  </option>

</options>
